      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sociologists view secularism as a shift in where social order is derived; the consensus on moral values moves from divine revelation to human reason and state law. While some research suggests that the loss of traditional religious communities can lead to social fragmentation, others argue that secular civil society can replace this function. Secular structures, like educational systems or volunteer organizations, can effectively provide similar forms of civic engagement and social support, maintaining cohesion through shared secular ideals. Therefore, cohesion isn't solely reliant on religious institutions, but rather on the presence of strong, shared belief systems.",
